<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boxer Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boxer Uprising, Yihetuan Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boxer Rebellion was an anti-foreign and anti-Christian uprising in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> led by the Yihetuan, the 'Militia United in Righteousness', known in the West as the Boxers for their martial rituals. Spreading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and Zhili in 1899 and 1900, the Boxers killed missionaries and thousands of Chinese Christians, and in June 1900, after the Qing court under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> declared war on the foreign powers, they besieged the foreign legations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. An international force of eight nations relieved the legations in August 1900 and occupied the capital, and the Boxer Protocol of 1901 imposed a huge indemnity on China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion.jpg" alt="Boxer Rebellion" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taku_Forts_Landing.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>, villagers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> feared the growth of foreign spheres of influence and resented Christian missionaries, who ignored local customs and used their power to protect their followers in court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, where the Righteous and Harmonious Fists arose, had long been troubled by social unrest, religious sects and martial societies. In 1897 and 1898 drought and floods forced farmers into the cities, and the movement blamed these disasters on foreign and Christian influence. American missionaries probably first called its members Boxers, because of the martial arts they practised. Their main rite was a spiritual possession with whirling swords and incantations, and they claimed invulnerability to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, rifle shot and blade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1899 the movement spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, destroying foreign property such as railways and attacking or murdering Chinese Christians and missionaries. In January 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, with a majority of conservatives at court, issued edicts in its defence. In the spring it moved north towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, burning churches and intimidating officials. Diplomats, missionaries, soldiers and some Chinese Christians took refuge in the Legation Quarter, which the Boxers besieged.</p><p>On 17 June 1900 the Eight-Nation Alliance, of American, Austro-Hungarian, British, French, German, Italian, Japanese and Russian troops, stormed the Dagu Forts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>. On 21 June Cixi issued a decree that was in effect a declaration of war on the powers. Officials were divided, and those in the southern provinces ignored the order to fight. The Alliance defeated the Imperial Army at Tianjin and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 14 August, ending the 55-day siege of the Legations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Looting of the capital and countryside followed, together with summary executions of suspected Boxers. The Boxer Protocol of 7 September 1901 required the execution of officials who had supported the movement, allowed foreign troops to be stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and imposed an indemnity of 450 million taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, more than the government's annual tax revenue, to be paid over 39 years to the eight nations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>'s handling of the rebellion further weakened its credibility and its control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It led to the Late Qing reforms and, to a greater extent, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boxer Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The disastrous <b>Boxer Uprising</b> of 1900 brought foreign occupation of Beijing and a crushing indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat discredited the policy of borrowing techniques alone and led reformers to demand changes in institutions, in the Hundred Days’ Reform of 1898 and, after the <b>Boxer Rebellion</b>, in the New Policies of the 1900s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
